Figure: OChem_01_04_001

Context:
This figure sets a geometric solid beside a chemical structure to establish a drawing convention, using four images across one horizontal band. At the left are two magenta line drawings of a four-faced solid: the first a plain outline with one front edge thickened, the second adding black interior edges, one solid and one dashed, that run from the top vertex and a side vertex to the center. The pair is labeled "A regular tetrahedron". At the right, a carbon written as "C" carries four hydrogens written as "H": one directly above and one to the right on ordinary thin lines, one to the left on a short dashed line, and one below on a heavy black wedge that widens away from the carbon. Three leader arrows name these bond types as "Bonds in plane of page", "Bond receding into page", and "Bond coming out of plane". A ball-and-stick model at the far right shows a dark gray central sphere with four pale hydrogen spheres on light rods, and a curly brace groups the notation and the model under "A tetrahedral carbon atom". Aligning solid, flat notation, and model lets the reader read wedges and dashes as depth cues rather than as different kinds of bonds, the shorthand used for three-dimensional structures throughout organic chemistry.

The previous interaction plan had issues.
Plan-level fixes requested by the critic:
  • Do not split the figure into 'Geometric' and 'Chemical' tabs; the source figure explicitly compares the geometric tetrahedron side-by-side with the chemical representations to show their equivalence. Plan for all three models to be visible simultaneously.

Generation-level issues observed (symptoms that may trace back to the plan):
  • Fix the spatial layout of the `draw2D` and `model3D` groups so they are clearly side-by-side without overlapping (ensure their positions correctly translate their children)
  • Correct the bracket geometry so it properly underlines both the 2D and 3D chemical representations rather than causing bounding-box/camera issues
  • Adjust the camera view and framing so that both the 2D and 3D models are distinctly visible on screen at the same time

Specific scores:
  • Overall: 2.4/5
  • Concept accuracy: 3/5
  • Interaction-Broken
  • Layout-Overlap

Revise the interaction plan to address these issues.
Focus on:
  • Ensuring all required interactions are explicitly specified
  • Clarifying the core concept that is being illustrated
  • Specifying demo steps that progressively build understanding
Output ONLY valid JSON (no markdown, no explanation).

